            <ArticleTitle>Breast cancer survivorship issues.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>805-27, ix</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.hoc.2013.05.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0889-8588(13)00057-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Survivors of breast cancer are confronted with a plethora of cancer treatment-related long-term symptoms, the most common being fatigue, hot flashes, sexual dysfunction, arthralgias, neuropathy, and cognitive dysfunction. Survivors of breast cancer also face cancer treatment-related disease states, such as osteoporosis, cardiac dysfunction, obesity, infertility, and secondary cancers. Evidence-based recommendations for screening, prevention, and early intervention should be implemented to improve quality of life and decrease comorbidities in this population.</AbstractText>
